direction: down

title: |md
  ## Three offload technologies, three boundaries
| {near: top-center; shape: text}

tso: "TSO — TCP Segmentation Offload" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
  tso_body: |md
    Where: NIC hardware (or driver software).

    Stack hands one large skb (up to 64 KB)
    to the driver. NIC chops into MSS-sized
    TCP segments, replicates the IP/TCP
    header per segment, computes checksums.

    ✅ Massive CPU savings on TX
    ❌ NIC must support it (NETIF_F_TSO)
  |
}

gso: "GSO — Generic Segmentation Offload" {
  style.fill: "#dcfce7"
  gso_body: |md
    Where: software, late in the TX path.

    If NIC doesn't do TSO, kernel does the
    chopping itself just before xmit. So the
    stack still runs TX once per superpacket;
    only the final segmentation is per-segment.

    ✅ Works on any NIC
    ✅ Same CPU win as TSO for the stack
    ❌ Software cost for the segmentation step

    See net/core/gso.c (added 2024).
  |
}

gro: "GRO — Generic Receive Offload" {
  style.fill: "#dbeafe"
  gro_body: |md
    Where: software in NAPI poll, before stack.

    Coalesces consecutive same-flow segments
    into one large skb (up to ~64 KB) so the
    stack sees one packet instead of N.

    ✅ Massive CPU savings on RX
    ❌ Adds latency (waits for more in burst)
    ❌ Sometimes interferes with timing

    See net/core/gro.c.
  |
}

ctrl: "Control:\nethtool -K eth0 tso on / off\nethtool -K eth0 gso on / off\nethtool -K eth0 gro on / off" {
  style.fill: "#fef3c7"
}

tso -> ctrl
gso -> ctrl
gro -> ctrl
